WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.479
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:04.535 --> 00:00:06.910
CECILE LEROUX: So when it
comes to some of the challenges

00:00:06.910 --> 00:00:11.590
in rolling out and actually
embracing a new approach

00:00:11.590 --> 00:00:15.700
that really stresses how people
can bring their authentic self

00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:17.830
to work, and it
required us to change,

00:00:17.830 --> 00:00:20.890
for example, our approach
to talent acquisition

00:00:20.890 --> 00:00:24.350
or recruiting, as one example--

00:00:24.350 --> 00:00:29.510
it's not OK to have limited
interview pools, for example.

00:00:29.510 --> 00:00:32.290
We had to make sure
that we demanded more.

00:00:32.290 --> 00:00:35.980
So often-- and I'm sure
anyone who's hearing this,

00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:37.840
it's like, oh, well,
we couldn't find enough

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:40.148
of x, y, or z candidates.

00:00:40.148 --> 00:00:41.690
Well, then you've
got to look harder.

00:00:41.690 --> 00:00:43.720
And so that means
taking a second look

00:00:43.720 --> 00:00:48.610
at where and how we actually
approach and describe

00:00:48.610 --> 00:00:49.790
our organization.

00:00:49.790 --> 00:00:52.070
So this emphasis
has to be authentic.

00:00:52.070 --> 00:00:53.840
It has to be true.

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:56.200
And so when we put together
interview teams, when

00:00:56.200 --> 00:00:59.110
we think about
development programs,

00:00:59.110 --> 00:01:01.270
sponsorship programs--
it's not just mentorship,

00:01:01.270 --> 00:01:04.209
but sponsorship programs--
we're always thinking about

00:01:04.209 --> 00:01:07.240
and we're always
including what will this

00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:09.280
mean to diverse candidates?

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:11.530
What outcomes will
they want to have?

00:01:11.530 --> 00:01:14.140
How do we understand
cultural differences

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:17.180
and actually embrace those
as part of what we do?

00:01:17.180 --> 00:01:19.180
And that's how you make
it part of your DNA.

00:01:19.180 --> 00:01:21.940
So there were challenges
in overcoming.

00:01:21.940 --> 00:01:23.708
You can't just go to
the usual suspects.

00:01:23.708 --> 00:01:25.000
You've got to look differently.

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:26.333
You've got to think differently.

00:01:26.333 --> 00:01:31.030
We've seen a two-fold increase
in different categories.

00:01:31.030 --> 00:01:33.220
In some of our
regions, for example,

00:01:33.220 --> 00:01:34.690
we've actually
doubled the number

00:01:34.690 --> 00:01:38.650
of women in technology, which
is a great result that we're

00:01:38.650 --> 00:01:39.670
really excited about.

00:01:39.670 --> 00:01:43.270
We look to other
organizations that we can also

00:01:43.270 --> 00:01:44.540
model and learn from.

00:01:44.540 --> 00:01:48.920
And so I do want to thank
Google for recognizing us,

00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:52.070
not only for our
diversity, equity,

00:01:52.070 --> 00:01:56.440
and our belonging efforts, but
also for our new technology

00:01:56.440 --> 00:01:58.870
approach, Life-Work
Technology, that

00:01:58.870 --> 00:02:03.190
has allowed us to not just
collaborate but really advance

00:02:03.190 --> 00:02:06.850
UKG and take a different
approach, not just to how

00:02:06.850 --> 00:02:09.639
we support our customers,
but also how we work

00:02:09.639 --> 00:02:13.240
and how we come and present
ourselves in the marketplace.

00:02:13.240 --> 00:02:16.990
So it's a huge honor
for us, but one

00:02:16.990 --> 00:02:21.340
that we are excited to
earn again and continue

00:02:21.340 --> 00:02:24.170
to advance our relationship and
our partnership with Google.

00:02:24.170 --> 00:02:26.350
So thank you so much,
Google, for these awards.

00:02:26.350 --> 00:02:29.700
[MUSIC PLAYING]

